  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe pattern remains active over the next few days with persistent\nlongwave troughing over the region and several shortwaves and \nstrong jet energy. Intermittent low rain chances will stick around\nthroughout the week, but the greatest chances concern a system \nanticipated late Tuesday into Wednesday. \n\nLingering showers in the near term will diminish as a shortwave \nridge works its way across our region tonight and Monday. But the \ndrier weather will be short-lived as the next system takes shape \nMonday night. A shortwave trough will dig from over the Pacific \nNW and phase with subtropical jet energy to aid in the lee \ncyclogenesis off the Rockies. The corresponding surface low will \nrapidly deepen as it tracks across the great plains. This should \ncreate increasingly breezy conditions from later Monday into \nTuesday as the pressure gradient tightens. Increasing moisture \nreturn and ascent will lead to storms developing along the front \nwest over Oklahoma and Texas, and these will push into our Tues\naftn/evening as the front approaches. The convection will become \nmore organized into a linear convective system that should move \nacross our forecast area Tue night.\n\nWith that said, lightning probabilities continue to suggest \nweakening of the system with eastward extent, which makes sense \nas low level forcing and instability weaken. The airmass will be\ncharacterized by a deep warm cloud depth and anomalous deep layer\nmoisture, so expect another high-precip efficiency environment to\nbe in place. There are differences between this upcoming event \nand what we just experienced. The potential to see rainfall that \nis more widespread is greater given the uniform, progressive lift,\nand this should keep amounts from becoming overly significant.\nLocally heavy rainfall could still yield localized FF issues and\nwill keep our current messaging of a limited flood threat going.\n\nThere is a low end chance (marginal risk) for severe weather as \nwell with this system over western portions of the forecast area. \nLinear storm mode and modest instability suggest primarily an \nisolated damaging wind gust risk, and the REFS guidance along with\nother near term high res guidance suggest the risk will be more \nconfined to northwest portions of the forecast area, right\nalong/west of the MS River.\n\nDue to the secondary low development into Wednesday, moisture wrap \naround lingers, so low (15-20%) rain chances continue through \nThursday. Additional rainfall amounts to generally under a tenth of \nan inch. The jet remains active and persistent moist trajectories \nmay keep isolated rain chances around to end the week into next \nweekend. We could see substantially cooler temperatures by next \nSunday as global ensembles show good agreement for significant \ncentral/eastern CONUS troughing. /SAS/EC/",
